# renki-config. Design

The contracts a tool's configuration is declared, read and written through,
and the rules by which a manifest's layers are found, ordered and merged. The
launcher resolves every setting once from five places in a fixed order and
hands the engine the result, the way it hands over `--dir`; a gui, a cli and a
hand edit all go through one schema and one file. This crate is the schema,
the store contract, the layer rules, the predicate and the resolver, and
nothing else: it reads no file and no environment, runs no process, allocates
nothing, borrows every string from text the caller holds, and speaks notko's
`Outcome` and `Maybe`. What reads the disk is `renki-manifest`, which
implements the store and asks this crate every question of rule.

## Kinds, as types

`Kind` is a trait over marker types, with a borrowed associated value type
`Value<'a>`, a `NAME` the schema prints, and three associated functions: from
text, as a flag or a variable carries it; from a `Literal`, as a store hands
it over; and back to a `Literal` for a store to write. Every kind is stateless
by construction, nothing on it is `self`. The kinds that ship: `Bool` to
`bool`; `Int` to `i64`; `Text` and `PathText` to `&'a str`, the second
refusing an empty one; `List<K>` to an iterator over `K`'s values, the
store's own iterator through an associated type rather than a `Vec`, whose
canonical text is `["a", "b"]` and which therefore refuses a text item
holding a `"`, since the canonical form has no escape for one; `Choice<C>` to
a `&'static str` drawn from `C::OPTIONS`, where `C: Choices` is a marker a
tool declares per closed set with the `choices!` macro; and `PredicateName`,
text that must name a predicate the manifest defines, for a field of a tool's
own schema that applies conditionally.

`Literal<'a, St>` is the seam between a store and a kind: `Bool`, `Int`,
`Str` over text the store lends already decoded, and `List` over the store's
own handle for the list, `St::List<'a>`, walked through the store's `items`;
a list has no text of its own to be read again, so an array of tables is one
like any other. A store turns its node into one, a kind turns one
into its value, and neither knows the other. `BadValue` is the refusal,
carrying the kind's name and the text offered.

## Scopes, as types

`Scope` is a trait over `User`, `Repo` and `Either`, closed by the private
`ConfigSealed`. Each says whether the person's file and the repository's file
are read for a setting of that scope. A repository file carrying a person's
setting is refused by name from the table the declaration filled in, rather
than by a check somebody remembered to write. Every layer of the repository's
file is the repository's file for this: a person's setting in
`name.local.ext` beside a repository's `name.ext` is refused the same.

## The setting and its row

`Setting<K, S>` is one setting, typed: a dotted key, a default in the kind's
text form, and one sentence of documentation. Its `row` is a `const fn`
producing `Declared<'static, St>`, the erased shape a `Tool` carries in one
static slice whatever the kinds of its settings: the kind's and scope's names,
the scope's two booleans, the default, the doc, and the kind's two parsers as
function pointers, so the row still checks a value the way its type would.

`Declared<'a, St>` borrows its strings for `'a`, so a table read at run time,
a tool descriptor's, is the same type over the descriptor's text. Such a row
is built by `Declared::named`, which takes the kind by its `NAME` and refuses a
name no shipped kind answers to; `Choice` is not among them, since its options
come from a type. Between `Setting::row` and `Declared::named` the pointers
and the names cannot disagree. `Declared::defect` is the descriptor check: a
key that is not a dotted identifier, a key under `meta`, which is the reader's
and no tool's, two rows sharing a key, or a default the row's own kind
refuses.

## The store

`Store` is the backend contract, implemented by marker types. It names the
format and its extension, parses text into a borrowed `Document<'a>` over an
index the caller hands in with the text, `St::Cell` slots, so a store keeps no
memory of its own and allocates nothing, answers
a dotted key as a `Literal` or `Isnt`, iterates the keys a document holds so
an unknown one is refused by name, tells what shape a key holds (`Shape`), and
writes one key into text it is given, into a `core::fmt::Write` sink, keeping
everything else in that text byte for byte, comments included. An array of
tables is a list here like any other, and its keys are `name.0.field` in the
document's order. TOML is the store that ships, in `renki-manifest`, which
reads the disk; the contracts name no format. A second store is an impl of
this trait and nothing more.

## Layers

A manifest is a base file `name.ext` and any number of layers
`name.<word>.ext`. `layer_of` reads a file name against the base's `name` and
the store's extension and answers its `Part`, `Base` or `Layer(LayerName)`,
or `Isnt` for a file that is no part of this manifest, and refuses with
`BadLayer` a file of the manifest's shape that cannot be one: two words, the
word `base`, or a word outside `[a-z0-9_-]`. `LayerName` is the word,
borrowed; `defaults` is the one that sits below the base.

`Order` is the place of every overlay above the base. It is read from the
base's `meta.overlays.order`, else the `defaults` layer's, else it is
`Order::DEFAULT`, `["*", "local", "secret", "private"]`, where `*` stands for
every layer the list does not name, in byte order of the word. `Order::rank`
answers a word's place, or a `BadLayer` naming the word and saying to add it
or `*` to `meta.overlays.order` where the order has no `*` and does not name
it. An order naming `defaults` or `base`, naming a word twice, or holding two
`*`, is refused when read.

## The meta table

`meta` is the one table read before the rest of any layer, and the only one
whose schema is this crate's. `Meta<'d, St>` reads it out of a document
through the store: `include` (a list of entries, each a glob matching files or
a directory where it ends in `/`, relative to the file that names it),
`order`, `untracked` (the words that must not be tracked by git, default
`local`, `secret` and `private`), `when` (a predicate's name), `predicates`,
`replace` and `unset` (dotted keys of this layer). `Meta::defect` refuses a
`meta` key that is none of these, an `order`, `untracked` or `predicates` in a
layer above the base, and a `when` on the base or on `defaults`, each by name.
`unknown_keys` and the resolver never see a key under `meta`.

`glob_matches` is the one glob rule both readers keep: `*`, `?` and `[...]`
within one path segment, with `!` negating a bracket, which is the POSIX
shell's pattern rule, and no `**`.

## Merging

`meet` is the merge rule, once, for every reader that merges: given the
`Shape` a lower layer holds at a key, the shape a higher one holds, and
whether the higher's `meta.replace` names the key, it answers the `Meet`:
`Deep` for two tables, which merge key by key; `Append` for two lists, the
higher's items after the lower's, an array of tables the same; `Over` for two
scalars, or for anything the higher replaces; or a `BadMerge` for one shape
over another that nothing replaces, naming both. `meta.unset` takes a key out
of what the layers below hold before the layer applies. A key `replace` names
that the layer does not hold, or `unset` names that no lower layer holds, is
refused.

## The stack

A `Layer<'a, St>` is one read layer: its `LayerName` or the base, a label for
the file it came from, and its document. A stack is a slice of them, lowest
first, which the caller builds and this crate only reads. `stacked` answers a
dotted key over a stack by `meet`: the highest layer holding a scalar wins;
for a list it answers `ListRun<'a, St>`, the run of each holding layer's list
from the lowest that the highest `replace` and `unset` leave, which the kind
walks layer by layer through the store's `items`, so an appended list is
neither copied nor allocated.

## The predicate

`Predicate<'a>` is one definition under `meta.predicates.<name>`: `Env` over a
variable's name, `Shell` over a command, and `Not`, `All` and `Any` over other
predicates' names. `Predicates<'d, St>` is the table read from the base and
`defaults`, the two layers always read, so no predicate depends on a layer it
decides; a definition in an overlay is refused by name.

`Answers` is what only the caller can give: a variable's value, and a shell
command's exit, run in the manifest's directory. `holds` evaluates a name over
the table and the caller's `Answers`: `Env` holds where `truthy` says the
value is (`1`, `true`, `yes`, `on`, in any case), does not where it is unset,
empty, `0`, `false`, `no` or `off`, and is a `BadPredicate` naming the
variable and the value otherwise; `Shell` holds on exit 0, not on 1, and is a
`BadPredicate` with the command and the exit on anything else. A name the
table does not define, and a loop among `Not`, `All` and `Any`, are refused
with the name and the loop. Nothing is kept between calls: a predicate is
evaluated when its use site is read, every time. This crate names no variable
and defines no predicate.

## Resolution and provenance

`Origin` is the closed set of places a value comes from: `Flag`, `Env`,
`Repo`, `User`, `Default`, in that precedence. `resolve` is generic over the
store and over how the caller answers a flag and a variable by key, takes the
repository's stack and the person's stack, each possibly empty, and yields per
setting a `ResolvedView<'a, St>`: the key, the value, the origin that won and,
for `Repo` and `User`, the layer, so `config get` prints `repo:local` where
the value came from `name.local.ext`. Its `Value<'a, St>` is text, a literal,
or a `ListRun`. A key present in a layer that no row declares is refused by
name and layer; a user-only key in any layer of the repository's stack is
refused by name and layer; a value a kind refuses is refused with its origin
and layer named. `env_key` renders a setting's variable name,
`<SHORT>_CFG_<KEY>` with dots to underscores, into a caller's sink, so the
launcher and an engine in rust agree on it without either allocating.

## What stays with the readers

Reading files, listing directories, the environment and processes, git, the
TOML store and its parser, deserialising a stack, the `--cfg` flag, the
`config` query's five verbs, `$EDITOR`, and the `String` diagnostics the
operator reads. The i/o and the store live in `renki-manifest`, `no_std` like
this crate; the flag, the verbs, the editor and the diagnostics in `renki`,
which is `std`. None of it is reachable from here: the forbidden-imports lint
refuses `renki::*` and `renki_manifest::*` in this crate, and `no-std` and
`no-alloc` refuse a file or a process reached any other way.
